course: dictionary
lesson: 12805
title: Eligibility: who is allowed?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Eligibility is about whether you are allowed to do or get something. You have eligibility when you meet the rules. «To join the under-11 football team, you must be under 11 years old.» If you are 10, you are eligible. If you are 12, you are not.
explain: Eligibility is a noun, and it often goes with «for»: «eligibility for free school meals». Look for the rules in the sentence. Rules are often about age, address or qualifications, so ask: what does this rule say?
explain: «Before you enter the science fair, check your eligibility on the form.» Check your eligibility means see if you qualify. Read the rules first, then decide if you can take part.
fact: Eligibility means having the right to do or get something because you meet the rules.
fact: The rules can be about your age, your address, or your qualifications, such as passing a test.
fact: «Eligibility rules» are the rules that say who can do something.
fact: «Check eligibility» means find out whether you qualify.
fact: Someone who meets the rules is eligible; the adjective «eligible» means the same as having eligibility.
exercise: «To enter the swimming race, you must be 10 or older.» Who has eligibility to enter? | A child who is 9 and has no swimming hat | *A child who is 10 | A child who is 8
exercise: What does eligibility mean? | *Having the right to do something because you meet the rules | Being very fast at running | A type of school bag | Losing a game on purpose
exercise: Which phrase means see if you qualify? | *Check eligibility | Ignore the form | Check the bus time | Copy the answers
test: Which of these is an example of an eligibility rule? | *Being the right age for the club | Having a favourite book | Owning a dog | Liking the colour blue
test: Mia reads «Check your eligibility before you apply.» What should she do? | Sign the form without reading it | Post the form without the rules | Ask for a bigger form | *Read the rules and see if she meets them
exam: «The club only takes children aged 10 and under. Jayden is 11. Is he eligible?» | *No, he is not eligible, because he does not meet the age rule | Yes, because he is older than the rule | Yes, because he likes the club | No, because clubs are boring
